Anchor Anti-Drift Custodian with Drift Quotient Sidecar
Executive summary
This project reshapes the existing cursor-anchor repository from a generic Cursor plugin marketplace template into Anchor: a single Cursor plugin whose mission is to prevent autonomous coding drift.
Anchor operates per opted-in repository. Once a repo is opted in, Anchor creates and supervises a durable Cursor cloud agent whose role is to keep the project aligned with its stated trajectory. Drift means:
1. Red CI/CD that nobody is fixing.
2. Loss of trajectory — the team or agent forgets what is being built, why it matters, or which phase is active.
3. PR-level evasion — submitters pad, hand-wave, create placeholders, rat-hole into infra, or change the goalposts instead of finishing the stated task.
4. Structural degradation — spaghetti code, clone-heavy patches, hallucinated APIs, and test-loop hacking.
5. The Orthogonal Quality Trap — code that is technically high quality but builds the wrong thing.
The system has two tightly connected parts:
1. Anchor Cursor Plugin — the control plane, user-facing commands, rules, skills, agents, hooks, MCP server, Node CLI, Cursor SDK integration, GitHub integration, trajectory store, and plan custodian.
2. Drift Quotient Sidecar — a black-box evaluation pipeline that monitors autonomous coding agents only through their artifacts: Git diffs, commit metadata, CI/CD logs, import resolution, test/source change ratios, AST complexity, clone density, and commit/CI failure patterns.
The minimum viable version must work with no external infrastructure: install the plugin, run /anchor-opt-in in a repo, and Anchor begins maintaining local state under ~/.anchor/<repo-slug>/. Webhook reception, dashboards, hosted daemons, and persistent services are optional v1 extensions, not required for the core workflow.

1. Unified product architecture
1.1 Product identity
The repository becomes a single-plugin marketplace repo for Anchor.
Anchor is not a collection of sample plugins. It is a focused anti-drift system for Cursor-assisted development.
The plugin:
* Maintains a living plan.md per opted-in repo.
* Records a JSONL trajectory database per opted-in repo.
* Attaches or creates a durable Cursor cloud agent per repo.
* Watches GitHub Actions and treats red CI as an outage.
* Audits PRs for evasion, verbosity mismatch, infra rat-holing, placeholder work, and plan drift.
* Computes a Drift Quotient using black-box sidecar metrics.
* Escalates severe or sustained drift into quarantine-style enforcement.
1.2 Runtime components
Anchor consists of these layers:
Layer	Responsibility
Cursor plugin manifest	Marketplace identity, logo, plugin metadata
Rules	Always-on and contextual behavior constraints for anti-drift work
Skills	SOPs for drift detection, CI repair, PR auditing, plan curation, trajectory recording
Agents	Persona prompts for custodian, CI fixer, PR enforcer, and plan archaeologist
Commands	/anchor-* user interface for opt-in, status, sync, CI repair, PR audit, planning, and opt-out
Hooks	Lightweight session and edit telemetry, drift warnings, and trajectory summaries
Node CLI	Operational core used by commands and hooks
Cursor SDK wrapper	Creates, reattaches, and messages durable Cursor cloud agents
GitHub integration	Reads Actions runs, PRs, diffs, logs, and posts comments
MCP server	Gives the cloud agent programmatic access to Anchor state and tools
Drift heuristics engine	Lightweight PR and branch-level practical drift checks
Drift Quotient sidecar	Statistical black-box monitoring pipeline with CUSUM, nursery, monitoring, quarantine, and re-baselining
Local state	~/.anchor/<repo-slug>/state.json, trajectory.jsonl, plan.md, run logs, cache, and sidecar baselines
1.3 The North Star document
Anchor’s per-repo plan.md is the project’s North Star document.
It lives at:

~/.anchor/<repo-slug>/plan.md

It contains:

## Mission

## Phases

## Active phase

## Recent decisions

The Drift Quotient sidecar uses this document as the canonical roadmap when computing semantic divergence and prerequisite alignment.
Humans may edit it freely. Anchor and the cloud agent may regenerate it through /anchor-plan --regenerate when no reliable plan exists or when the project needs re-baselining.

